{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786439874,"nonce":"hEQFYP5vrYnhXlTn0yFgWUXitwo=","title":"An alert with no rule snapshot answers 422, and the detail page shows 'Validation failed'","message":"**What is wrong.** `GET /api/v1/alerts/{id}/rule` returns **422 `rules_invalid_id`** — *\"a rule key's source id must be a UUID\"* — for any alert that has no rule snapshot bound. Absence of a snapshot is being reported as a **client validation error**.\n\nMeasured against a real server (schema 00039), five alerts ingested through the real webhook:\n\n```\nQueueBacklog     rule -\u003e HTTP 422\nCertExpiry       rule -\u003e HTTP 422\nMemLeak          rule -\u003e HTTP 422\nDiskFilling      rule -\u003e HTTP 422\nHighErrorRate    rule -\u003e HTTP 200      \u003c- the only one whose generatorURL carried g0.expr\n```\n\nSo the 200 path works; the no-snapshot path is the defect. The four 422s are alerts whose `generatorURL` had no `g0.expr`, which is entirely ordinary — a Grafana-sourced alert, a hand-fired alert, an Alertmanager whose Prometheus is unreachable, or any alert captured as `unavailable` all land here.\n\n**Why it matters.** This is the product's headline promise failing in the place a user looks for it. `README.md` leads with *\"what the rule said at that moment\"* and ADR 0009 makes fire-time snapshotting **the** differentiator against Grafana's alert history. On the alert detail page the *Rule at fire time* panel renders the 422 as a red **\"Validation failed — a rule key's source id must be a UUID\"** box with a *Try again* button and a request id.\n\nThree things are wrong with that, in increasing order of seriousness: it is not a validation failure and nothing the user did caused it; the message is internal vocabulary that means nothing to an operator; and *Try again* invites a retry that can never succeed. A user reading it reasonably concludes oto is broken, when the truthful statement is *\"oto captured no rule for this alert\"* — which is a normal outcome the UI already knows how to render, since the alert list shows a plain `—` in its Rule column for these same four alerts.\n\n**Where.** `internal/rules/api/` (the `/alerts/{id}/rule` and `/occurrences/{id}/rule` handlers and the rule-key parsing that produces `rules_invalid_id`), `internal/rules/service/`, and the *Rule at fire time* panel in `web/src/features/alerts/detail/`.\n\n**Done when.** An alert with no rule snapshot answers with something that says so — a 404, or a 200 carrying an explicit \"unavailable\" — never a 422; the detail panel renders that as the same calm absence the list already shows rather than an error box; and a test covers an alert whose `generatorURL` carries no expression. Worth checking the occurrence variant (`/occurrences/{id}/rule`) at the same time, and whether the new batch endpoint from c1d05a7 has the same hole for ids it cannot resolve.\n\nFound by running the UI against a real backend for issue 4892848.","files":null}]}